{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AfuA8zXuBWAanJfQrddkNi","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Config, roles, and providers\n2\t\n3\t`src/config/`, `src/cli/config.ts`, `src/cli/resolve.ts`. Credential\n4\tlookup order is in `auth.md`.\n5\t\n6\t## Loading\n7\t\n8\t- `loadConfig(path)` parses `.roast.json` as JSONC with exact-case keys\n9\t  and unknown keys rejected. `version: 1` is required; a literal\n10\t  `api_key` is rejected. There are no built-in roles or models: a role\n11\t  missing from the config fails in `resolveRole`.\n12\t- `jsonc.ts` parses into null-prototype objects (`newObject` /\n13\t  `setKey`, so `__proto__` is an ordinary key). `schema.ts` (zod) checks\n14\t  shape and types only, and `load.ts` keeps the validated *input*, never\n15\t  zod's output (zod's record output turns a `__proto__` key into a\n16\t  prototype). Range and cross-field checks are plain functions in\n17\t  `load.ts` so the Go-era check order holds.\n18\t- `findConfig(start, stop)` walks up to `stop` (the Git toplevel),\n19\t  comparing directories after `realpath`: macOS temp dirs are\n20\t  `/var/...` while `git rev-parse --show-toplevel` prints\n21\t  `/private/var/...`.\n22\t- The CLI loads config only through `loadProjectConfig(explicit, ctx)`\n23\t  (`src/cli/config.ts`), which attaches the stored-credentials store. A\n24\t  missing config is a usage error naming `npx roast init`.\n25\t- Relative prompt-override paths resolve against the config file's\n26\t  directory: read prompts through `promptPath(cfg, ...)`, never the raw\n27\t  config string. The trust projection hashes the raw string, so\n28\t  approvals survive worktree moves.\n29\t\n30\t## Settings\n31\t\n32\tOnly credentials come from outside the config. Every other setting is\n33\tin it (`limits`, `interactive`, `gate`, `review.snapshot`); an explicit\n34\t0 differs from unset, and `load.ts` checks ranges. The CLI resolves\n35\tthem with `flagOrConfigInt` / `flagOrConfigDuration` (`resolve.ts`:\n36\tflag, then config, then default).\n37\t\n38\t## Schema and starter\n39\t\n40\t- `roast.schema.json` (repo root, shipped through `package.json`\n41\t  `files`) is generated: `npm run schema` renders\n42\t  `src/config/json-schema.ts` from the zod schema. Adding, removing, or\n43\t  retyping a config field changes the zod schema and the config types\n44\t  in the same change, then regenerates; never edit the JSON by hand.\n45\t  `test/config/schema.ts` fails on drift. Editor-only constraints (enum,\n46\t  pattern, minimum, required) go in the zod `.meta()`.\n47\t- The `roast init` starter (`starter.ts`) shows commented-out defaults\n48\t  from `defaults.ts`, the same module runtime settings use.\n49\t- `starter()` takes a `StarterChoice` per role -- either a `RoleChoice`\n50\t  (provider + model) for built-in providers or a `CustomChoice`\n51\t  (endpoint, protocol, apiKeyEnv, model) for custom endpoints.\n52\t  `starterFromModels()` is the backward-compat wrapper that maps model\n53\t  strings onto choices via `placeModel` / `otherProvider`.\n54\t  `choiceFromModel()` builds a `RoleChoice` from a flag value.\n55\t- `placeModel` places a model by its ID alone: a\n56\t  `<vendor>/<model>[:<variant>]` ID goes to OpenRouter, a bare ID to the\n57\t  provider `inferFamily` names. Custom model IDs skip `placeModel`\n58\t  entirely -- they stay on whatever custom endpoint the user typed. A\n59\t  placeholder role follows an OpenRouter role onto OpenRouter; otherwise\n60\t  it takes the other direct provider.\n61\t- Custom profile naming: a single custom role uses `custom`; when both\n62\t  roles are custom with different settings or models, they split to\n63\t  `custom-finder` and `custom-verifier`; identical settings share\n64\t  `custom`.\n65\t- Suggested model IDs for each built-in provider live in\n66\t  `SUGGESTED_MODELS` (`defaults.ts`). The `UnplaceableModelError`\n67\t  example text uses them so the two cannot drift.\n68\t- Interactive prompt flow lives in `src/cli/init.ts`:\n69\t  `promptRoleChoice` per role, calling `promptProvider` then\n70\t  `promptModel` (built-in) or `promptCustomEndpoint` (custom). The\n71\t  menu order is hardcoded in `PROVIDER_MENU` (Anthropic, OpenAI,\n72\t  OpenRouter, Custom endpoint). `suggestedModel()` picks the default\n73\t  from `SUGGESTED_MODELS`; for OpenRouter, it picks the slug for the\n74\t  vendor the other role is not using. A typed model at a built-in\n75\t  provider must pass `placeModel` and land on the chosen provider.\n76\t- When the finder chooses Custom, the verifier's default provider is\n77\t  also Custom, and its endpoint, protocol, and key variable prompts\n78\t  default to the finder's answers.\n79\t- After writing a config with custom endpoints, `init` offers trust\n80\t  approval (using `writeTrustSummary` and `promptNoDefault`) and prints\n81\t  a credential hint naming the variable to export.\n82\t\n83\t## Providers\n84\t\n85\t- `lookupProvider(name)` and `providerAt(endpoint, protocol)`\n86\t  (`provider.ts`) are the one place that says which endpoint,\n87\t  protocols, standard key variable, key page, and login method a\n88\t  provider has. Trust standardness, ambient credentials, and the `init`\n89\t  starter consult it; add a provider there, not in those callers. A\n90\t  provider's first protocol is the one starter profiles use.\n91\t- A new provider's key variable must be stripped from test\n92\t  environments: see `cleanEnv` in `typescript.md`.\n93\t- `defaultBaseURL(protocol)` stays separate: it is the implicit endpoint\n94\t  of a profile that omits one; OpenRouter is never one.\n95\t- A role that gets no ambient key reports any set provider key variable\n96\t  as withheld (`withheldKey`, `credential.ts`), preferring the\n97\t  protocol's default provider.\n98\t- `parseEndpoint` (`provider.ts`) is the one endpoint parser (treats\n99\t  `https:///v1` and `https:v1` as an empty host); `endpoint.ts` and\n100\t  `sameEndpoint` use it.\n101\t\n102\t## Roles (`role.ts`, `credential.ts`, `provider.ts`)\n103\t\n104\t- Resolution takes `env` explicitly and never reads `process.env`.\n105\t- `ResolvedRole` is frozen with a non-enumerable `apiKey` and `stored`,\n106\t  so JSON, spread, and `util.inspect` omit the secret. Change a role\n107\t  with `updateRole(role, changes)`; a `{...role}` spread drops the key.\n108\t- `StoredCredentials` lives in `credential.ts` (re-exported by\n109\t  `load.ts`).\n110\t\n111\t## Tests\n112\t\n113\t- Role tests build configs with `configFrom` / `resolveFinder` and the\n114\t  in-memory `FakeStore` in `test/config/helpers.ts`.\n115\t- Configs the tests load as realistic examples go in\n116\t  `test/config/examples.ts`; the schema test validates each with ajv.\n117\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"84d06975-53aa-44e0-a4d2-4df63462776f","uuid":"7ab96885-c56f-48ec-ad1c-434debd1986f","timestamp":"2026-11-24T23:27:40.930Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/nick/code/roast.review/docs/agents/config.md","content":"# Config, roles, and providers\n\n`src/config/`, `src/cli/config.ts`, `src/cli/resolve.ts`. Credential\nlookup order is in `auth.md`.\n\n## Loading\n\n- `loadConfig(path)` parses `.roast.json` as JSONC with exact-case keys\n  and unknown keys rejected. `version: 1` is required; a literal\n  `api_key` is rejected. There are no built-in roles or models: a role\n  missing from the config fails in `resolveRole`.\n- `jsonc.ts` parses into null-prototype objects (`newObject` /\n  `setKey`, so `__proto__` is an ordinary key). `schema.ts` (zod) checks\n  shape and types only, and `load.ts` keeps the validated *input*, never\n  zod's output (zod's record output turns a `__proto__` key into a\n  prototype). Range and cross-field checks are plain functions in\n  `load.ts` so the Go-era check order holds.\n- `findConfig(start, stop)` walks up to `stop` (the Git toplevel),\n  comparing directories after `realpath`: macOS temp dirs are\n  `/var/...` while `git rev-parse --show-toplevel` prints\n  `/private/var/...`.\n- The CLI loads config only through `loadProjectConfig(explicit, ctx)`\n  (`src/cli/config.ts`), which attaches the stored-credentials store. A\n  missing config is a usage error naming `npx roast init`.\n- Relative prompt-override paths resolve against the config file's\n  directory: read prompts through `promptPath(cfg, ...)`, never the raw\n  config string. The trust projection hashes the raw string, so\n  approvals survive worktree moves.\n\n## Settings\n\nOnly credentials come from outside the config. Every other setting is\nin it (`limits`, `interactive`, `gate`, `review.snapshot`); an explicit\n0 differs from unset, and `load.ts` checks ranges. The CLI resolves\nthem with `flagOrConfigInt` / `flagOrConfigDuration` (`resolve.ts`:\nflag, then config, then default).\n\n## Schema and starter\n\n- `roast.schema.json` (repo root, shipped through `package.json`\n  `files`) is generated: `npm run schema` renders\n  `src/config/json-schema.ts` from the zod schema. Adding, removing, or\n  retyping a config field changes the zod schema and the config types\n  in the same change, then regenerates; never edit the JSON by hand.\n  `test/config/schema.ts` fails on drift. Editor-only constraints (enum,\n  pattern, minimum, required) go in the zod `.meta()`.\n- The `roast init` starter (`starter.ts`) shows commented-out defaults\n  from `defaults.ts`, the same module runtime settings use.\n- `starter()` takes a `StarterChoice` per role -- either a `RoleChoice`\n  (provider + model) for built-in providers or a `CustomChoice`\n  (endpoint, protocol, apiKeyEnv, model) for custom endpoints.\n  `starterFromModels()` is the backward-compat wrapper that maps model\n  strings onto choices via `placeModel` / `otherProvider`.\n  `choiceFromModel()` builds a `RoleChoice` from a flag value.\n- `placeModel` places a model by its ID alone: a\n  `<vendor>/<model>[:<variant>]` ID goes to OpenRouter, a bare ID to the\n  provider `inferFamily` names. Custom model IDs skip `placeModel`\n  entirely -- they stay on whatever custom endpoint the user typed. A\n  placeholder role follows an OpenRouter role onto OpenRouter; otherwise\n  it takes the other direct provider.\n- Custom profile naming: a single custom role uses `custom`; when both\n  roles are custom with different settings or models, they split to\n  `custom-finder` and `custom-verifier`; identical settings share\n  `custom`.\n- Suggested model IDs for each built-in provider live in\n  `SUGGESTED_MODELS` (`defaults.ts`). The `UnplaceableModelError`\n  example text uses them so the two cannot drift.\n- Interactive prompt flow lives in `src/cli/init.ts`:\n  `promptRoleChoice` per role, calling `promptProvider` then\n  `promptModel` (built-in) or `promptCustomEndpoint` (custom). The\n  menu order is hardcoded in `PROVIDER_MENU` (Anthropic, OpenAI,\n  OpenRouter, Custom endpoint). `suggestedModel()` picks the default\n  from `SUGGESTED_MODELS`; for OpenRouter, it picks the slug for the\n  vendor the other role is not using. A typed model at a built-in\n  provider must pass `placeModel` and land on the chosen provider.\n- When the finder chooses Custom, the verifier's default provider is\n  also Custom, and its endpoint, protocol, and key variable prompts\n  default to the finder's answers.\n- After writing a config with custom endpoints, `init` offers trust\n  approval (using `writeTrustSummary` and `promptNoDefault`) and prints\n  a credential hint naming the variable to export.\n\n## Providers\n\n- `lookupProvider(name)` and `providerAt(endpoint, protocol)`\n  (`provider.ts`) are the one place that says which endpoint,\n  protocols, standard key variable, key page, and login method a\n  provider has. Trust standardness, ambient credentials, and the `init`\n  starter consult it; add a provider there, not in those callers. A\n  provider's first protocol is the one starter profiles use.\n- A new provider's key variable must be stripped from test\n  environments: see `cleanEnv` in `typescript.md`.\n- `defaultBaseURL(protocol)` stays separate: it is the implicit endpoint\n  of a profile that omits one; OpenRouter is never one.\n- A role that gets no ambient key reports any set provider key variable\n  as withheld (`withheldKey`, `credential.ts`), preferring the\n  protocol's default provider.\n- `parseEndpoint` (`provider.ts`) is the one endpoint parser (treats\n  `https:///v1` and `https:v1` as an empty host); `endpoint.ts` and\n  `sameEndpoint` use it.\n\n## Roles (`role.ts`, `credential.ts`, `provider.ts`)\n\n- Resolution takes `env` explicitly and never reads `process.env`.\n- `ResolvedRole` is frozen with a non-enumerable `apiKey` and `stored`,\n  so JSON, spread, and `util.inspect` omit the secret. Change a role\n  with `updateRole(role, changes)`; a `{...role}` spread drops the key.\n- `StoredCredentials` lives in `credential.ts` (re-exported by\n  `load.ts`).\n\n## Tests\n\n- Role tests build configs with `configFrom` / `resolveFinder` and the\n  in-memory `FakeStore` in `test/config/helpers.ts`.\n- Configs the tests load as realistic examples go in\n  `test/config/examples.ts`; the schema test validates each with ajv.\n","numLines":117,"startLine":1,"totalLines":117}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01AfuA8zXuBWAanJfQrddkNi","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
